𝟑𝟎 𝐃𝐚𝐲𝐬 𝐨𝐟 𝐀𝐧𝐬𝐢𝐛𝐥𝐞 – 𝐃𝐚𝐲 𝟏𝟐/𝟑𝟎

🔄 𝐀𝐧𝐬𝐢𝐛𝐥𝐞 𝐋𝐨𝐨𝐩𝐬 – 𝐒𝐭𝐨𝐩 𝐑𝐞𝐩𝐞𝐚𝐭𝐢𝐧𝐠 𝐭𝐡𝐞 𝐒𝐚𝐦𝐞 𝐓𝐚𝐬𝐤

Imagine you need to create 10 users on 100 servers.

Would you write the same Ansible task 10 times? ❌

Of course not!

This is where 𝐀𝐧𝐬𝐢𝐛𝐥𝐞 𝐋𝐨𝐨𝐩𝐬 make automation cleaner, shorter, and easier to maintain. 🔥

🔹 𝐁𝐚𝐬𝐢𝐜 𝐋𝐨𝐨𝐩 𝐄𝐱𝐚𝐦𝐩𝐥𝐞

- name: Install multiple packages
  package:
    name: "{{ item }}"
    state: present
  loop:
    - git
    - curl
    - vim
    - wget

Ansible runs the same task for every item in the list.

One task → Multiple packages. 🚀

🔹 𝐂𝐫𝐞𝐚𝐭𝐢𝐧𝐠 𝐌𝐮𝐥𝐭𝐢𝐩𝐥𝐞 𝐔𝐬𝐞𝐫𝐬

- name: Create users
  user:
    name: "{{ item }}"
    state: present
  loop:
    - devops
    - developer
    - tester
    - monitoring

Now Ansible creates all four users automatically.

🔹 𝐋𝐨𝐨𝐩 𝐖𝐢𝐭𝐡 𝐕𝐚𝐫𝐢𝐚𝐛𝐥𝐞𝐬

You can store the list in a variable and reuse it:

packages:
  - git
  - curl
  - vim

loop: "{{ packages }}"

This makes your playbooks more reusable and maintainable.

🌍 𝐑𝐞𝐚𝐥-𝐖𝐨𝐫𝐥𝐝 𝐄𝐱𝐚𝐦𝐩𝐥𝐞

When provisioning an application server, you may need:

✅ Git
✅ Docker
✅ Python
✅ Unzip
✅ Curl

Instead of writing five separate tasks, use one loop.

🔥 𝐎𝐧𝐞 𝐓𝐚𝐬𝐤 → 𝐌𝐮𝐥𝐭𝐢𝐩𝐥𝐞 𝐎𝐩𝐞𝐫𝐚𝐭𝐢𝐨𝐧𝐬

⚠️ 𝐂𝐨𝐦𝐦𝐨𝐧 𝐌𝐢𝐬𝐭𝐚𝐤𝐞

❌ Repeating the same task for every package or user.

✅ Use a 𝐥𝐨𝐨𝐩 to eliminate unnecessary duplication.

🎯 𝐊𝐞𝐲 𝐓𝐚𝐤𝐞𝐚𝐰𝐚𝐲

𝐀𝐧𝐬𝐢𝐛𝐥𝐞 𝐋𝐨𝐨𝐩𝐬 𝐟𝐨𝐥𝐥𝐨𝐰 𝐭𝐡𝐞 𝐃𝐑𝐘 𝐩𝐫𝐢𝐧𝐜𝐢𝐩𝐥𝐞 — 𝐃𝐨𝐧'𝐭 𝐑𝐞𝐩𝐞𝐚𝐭 𝐘𝐨𝐮𝐫𝐬𝐞𝐥𝐟.

They reduce duplicate code, improve readability, and make playbooks easier to maintain.
